.TH BORINGCA 1 "August 2026" "boringca 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boringca \- quickly create a CA and issue child certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boringca
.br
.B boringca
.I name
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B init
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B issue
.I name
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boringca
is a small, self-contained command-line tool to create a root
Certificate Authority and issue server/client certificates signed by
it, for local development, home lab and internal PKI use. All
certificate generation and signing happens in-process (rcgen, ring
backend); no external openssl binary or other tool is invoked at
runtime. Keys are ECDSA P-256. It is NOT a replacement for a
production CA/PKI system.
.PP
Run with no arguments, \fBboringca\fR sets up the CA on first use and
prints where it is stored. Run as \fBboringca\fR \fIname\fR, it issues a
certificate for DNS name \fIname\fR signed by that CA, creating the CA
first if it doesn't exist yet -- this is shorthand for \fBboringca
issue\fR \fIname\fR with every option left at its default. The \fBinit\fR
and \fBissue\fR subcommands below give full control over every option.
.SH INIT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-cn " name"
Common Name of the root CA (default: "BoringCA Root").
.TP
.BI \-\-days " n"
Validity in days (default: 3650).
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
CA store directory (default: \fB$BORINGCA_HOME\fR or \fI~/.boringca\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing CA in \fB\-\-dir\fR.
.SH ISSUE OPTIONS
.TP
.I name
Short name for the certificate, used to derive file names
(\fIprivate/name.key\fR, \fIcerts/name.crt\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-cn " name"
Common Name (default: the certificate's \fIname\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-san " list"
Comma-separated Subject Alternative Names, e.g.
\fIdns:example.com,dns:www.example.com,ip:10.0.0.1\fR
(default: \fIdns:<cn>\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-server
Issue a server certificate, \fIextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth\fR (default).
.TP
.B \-\-client
Issue a client certificate, \fIextendedKeyUsage=clientAuth\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-both
Issue a certificate valid for both server and client authentication.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " n"
Validity in days (default: 825).
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
CA store directory (default: \fB$BORINGCA_HOME\fR or \fI~/.boringca\fR).
.SH FILES
Under the CA store directory:
.RS
.TP
.I ca.key, ca.crt
Root CA private key and certificate.
.TP
.I ca.cn
The CA's Common Name, used to reconstruct it in memory when signing new
certificates. Do not edit or remove while the CA is in use.
.TP
.I private/<name>.key
Issued certificate's private key.
.TP
.I certs/<name>.crt
Issued certificate.
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORINGCA_HOME
Default CA store directory, used when \fB\-\-dir\fR is not given.
.SH TRUSTING THE CA
Every time the CA is created, boringca prints the
.BR update\-ca\-certificates (8)
command to trust it system-wide on Debian/Ubuntu:
.RS
.EX
sudo cp <ca.crt> /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/<name>.crt
sudo update-ca-certificates
.EE
.RE
.I <name>
is derived from the store directory's name, so certificates from
different \fB\-\-dir\fR stores don't collide. On other distributions, place
.I ca.crt
wherever locally-trusted CAs are expected instead (e.g.
.BR update\-ca\-trust (8)
on Fedora/RHEL), or import it into your browser/OS trust store directly.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
boringca init --cn "Home Lab CA"
boringca issue nas --san dns:nas.lan,ip:192.168.1.10
boringca issue laptop --client --cn "fabrice@laptop"
.EE
.SH AUTHOR
Fabrice Dagorn <fabrice@dagorn.fr>
